1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,129
NASA's Kepler space telescope revealed

2
00:00:02,159 --> 00:00:03,330
over three hundred million

3
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,645
rocky habitable worlds across our galaxy.

4
00:00:06,381 --> 00:00:07,783
Billions of older stars formed

5
00:00:07,813 --> 00:00:09,539
long before our Sun ignited.

6
00:00:09,752 --> 00:00:12,056
Even crawling at one percent of light speed,

7
00:00:12,166 --> 00:00:13,448
a starship fleet crosses the

8
00:00:13,478 --> 00:00:15,496
galaxy in ten million years.

9
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:18,132
In cosmic time, that's a blink—

10
00:00:18,242 --> 00:00:20,310
so physicist Enrico Fermi asked:

11
00:00:20,433 --> 00:00:21,486
where is everybody?

12
00:00:21,768 --> 00:00:24,152
Since nineteen sixty, giant radio dishes

13
00:00:24,182 --> 00:00:25,687
and optical telescopes have scanned

14
00:00:25,717 --> 00:00:27,871
thousands of nearby star systems.

15
00:00:28,625 --> 00:00:30,717
Across more than sixty years of listening,

16
00:00:30,828 --> 00:00:33,143
confirmed alien signals equal zero.

17
00:00:33,384 --> 00:00:35,815
To explain why alien life feels so rare,

18
00:00:35,962 --> 00:00:38,154
Robin Hanson proposed the Great Filter.

19
00:00:38,908 --> 00:00:40,759
Between dead rock and starflight sits

20
00:00:40,789 --> 00:00:43,797
at least one brutal barrier almost nothing survives.

21
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:45,713
If that filter lies behind us,

22
00:00:45,787 --> 00:00:47,817
we already hit the cosmic jackpot.

23
00:00:48,571 --> 00:00:50,707
On early Earth, simple microbes stalled

24
00:00:50,737 --> 00:00:52,105
for nearly two billion years

25
00:00:52,135 --> 00:00:54,809
before two cells merged into complex life.

26
00:00:55,053 --> 00:00:57,645
The darker option puts the Great Filter ahead of us.

27
00:00:58,390 --> 00:01:01,357
Perhaps rocky worlds sprout intelligent species often—

28
00:01:01,453 --> 00:01:02,845
only to wipe themselves out

29
00:01:02,875 --> 00:01:05,181
once nuclear or climate tech arrives.

30
00:01:05,412 --> 00:01:08,261
Still, radio silence doesn't prove we're alone:

31
00:01:08,352 --> 00:01:11,039
astronomers calculate our searches have sampled barely

32
00:01:11,069 --> 00:01:12,738
a glass from the cosmic ocean.

33
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:14,932
Until one signal breaks the quiet,

34
00:01:15,008 --> 00:01:17,053
Earth remains the only voice we know.

35
00:01:17,825 --> 00:01:20,484
Tonight, our telescopes keep listening across

36
00:01:20,514 --> 00:01:22,659
three hundred million silent worlds.
